<h2 id="scope">Start with one deliverable and its limits</h2>
<p>Consider a provider offering a metric-definition reconciliation workshop. The buyer brings two approved, redacted reports that use the same metric name but produce different totals. The provider documents the population, date window, inclusion rules, calculation, and source owner for each report. The output is a metric-definition register and a list of unresolved differences. It is not a data-platform rebuild, a financial audit, or a promise to increase revenue.</p>
<p>A plausible customer has a decision delayed by inconsistent reporting, a responsible operations or finance owner, and appropriate access to the definitions being compared. An organization that wants an autonomous prediction system has a different requirement. An organization whose reports already agree may have no reason to buy this workshop. Define the work before using industry, headcount, or software-stack filters.</p>
<h2 id="evidence">Use four evidence fields, with explicit unknowns</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Problem:</strong> the buyer confirms a reporting-definition disagreement relevant to a real decision.</li><li><strong>Owner:</strong> a named internal role can approve the definitions and involve the people who produce the reports.</li><li><strong>Access:</strong> the buyer confirms that approved, appropriately redacted definitions can be shared through the agreed process. Do not copy confidential data into a prospecting worksheet.</li><li><strong>Delivery fit:</strong> the provider can complete the bounded workshop within agreed capacity and scope.</li></ol>
<p>For each field, record confirmed, contradicted, or unknown, plus a source reference and review date. A contradicted field is still a completed evidence field: it supports a decision to stop or change scope. A public hiring announcement is a research observation; it does not confirm an internal reporting problem, access permission, or budget.</p>
<p>Keep buying readiness separate. A profile can fit the service while the buyer has no current evaluation priority. Record the buyer's next decision and timing only after they confirm them. A convenient quarter-end date in the seller's CRM does not establish buyer readiness.</p>
<h2 id="worked-review">Worked review: four fictional accounts</h2>
<div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Analytics account profiling example" tabindex="0"><table><caption>Each row uses the same four evidence fields</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Fictional account</th><th scope="col">Problem / owner / access / delivery</th><th scope="col">Disposition</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Practice Analytics A</th><td>Confirmed / confirmed / confirmed / confirmed. The buyer describes two definitions of qualified pipeline and agrees to a scoped review.</td><td>Fit confirmed; buyer requests a scope discussion. No purchase is assumed.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Practice Analytics B</th><td>Confirmed / confirmed / unknown / unknown. A reporting disagreement exists, but input authorization and delivery requirements remain unresolved.</td><td>Research. Resolve those two fields before advancing.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Practice Analytics C</th><td>Contradicted / confirmed / confirmed / confirmed. The buyer says the reports reconcile and identifies no remaining definition problem.</td><td>Outside this offer's current problem definition. Close this sales path unless the buyer raises a different relevant need.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Practice Analytics D</th><td>Unknown / unknown / unknown / unknown. Only a public job advertisement has been observed.</td><td>Research only. Ask whether the workflow is relevant; do not infer an opportunity.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The example contains one confirmed-fit account, two research accounts, and one account outside the defined problem: 1 + 2 + 1 = 4 reviewed accounts. The disposition is a record of the evidence available for this offer, not a permanent label attached to a company.</p>
<h2 id="measurement">Three measures answer different questions</h2>
<div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Account profile measurement calculations" tabindex="0"><table><caption>Reconcile the denominator before comparing results</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">Calculation for the example</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Evidence completeness</th><td>Account A has 4 known fields; B has 2; C has 4; D has 0. Total 10 known fields / (4 accounts × 4 fields) = 10/16 = 62.5%.</td><td>How much of the defined evidence has been resolved. Both confirmed and contradicted values count as known.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Confirmed-fit share of reviewed accounts</th><td>1 confirmed-fit account / 4 reviewed accounts = 25%.</td><td>What share of this exact review group meets every gate. Unknown accounts remain in the denominator.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Confirmed-fit share of fully evidenced accounts</th><td>Accounts A and C have all 4 fields resolved. 1 confirmed-fit account / 2 fully evidenced accounts = 50%.</td><td>Fit among completed reviews only. It excludes B and D, so it must not be presented as the fit share of all four accounts.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>These are descriptive calculations on fictional inputs. They do not measure response rate, purchase likelihood, realized revenue, or the accuracy of an analytics model. The two fit percentages differ because their denominators differ. An improved completeness rate could simply mean the rep documented more no-fit findings.</p>
<h2>Use a metric-definition disagreement as discovery evidence</h2>
<p>Suppose one fictional report includes opportunities created during a month, while another includes opportunities that entered a qualified stage during that month. A difference in totals can follow from the population definitions without either report being technically broken. Ask the buyer which decision each report supports before promising a single replacement number.</p>
<p>Record the entity counted, event date, reporting window, stage rule, duplicate treatment, excluded records, source system, and approving owner. If the buyer cannot approve a common definition yet, the workshop can document the disagreement and its owner. Do not advertise a completed reconciliation when the decision remains open.</p>
